+++
title: Gutians
description: People of ancient west Asia
hook: Someone ruled southern Mesopotamia after the Akkadian Empire fell, and
  almost nothing about them can be recovered. No artifact has been positively
  identified as Gutian, and their language survives only in the names of their
  kings.
standfirst: The Guti were a people of the ancient Near East who both appeared
  and disappeared during the Bronze Age. They left no identified material
  culture and no readable language, and yet, the Sumerian king list says, they
  ruled Sumer for several generations after Akkad fell.
key-facts:
  - name: Who they were
    value: A people of the ancient Near East who both appeared and disappeared
      during the Bronze Age
  - name: Homeland
    value: "Gutium (Sumerian: 𒄖𒌅𒌝𒆠, Gutūm^KI, or 𒄖𒋾𒌝𒆠, Gutium^KI)"
  - name: They appear around
    value: 2200 BC
  - name: And are gone by
    value: 2116 BC
  - name: Language
    value: Unverifiable — the Gutian language lacks a text corpus apart from some
      proper names
  - name: Best known for
    value: Overrunning southern Mesopotamia and forming the short-lived Gutian
      dynasty of Sumer
pull-quotes:
  - text: Gutium, the fanged snake of the mountain ranges, a people who acted
      violently against the gods, people who the kingship of Sumer to the
      mountains took away, who Sumer with wickedness filled
    attribution: Victory Stele of Utu-hengal
    section: decline-from-the-late-22nd-century-bce-onwards
  - text: The Gutians were unhappy people unaware how to revere the gods, ignorant
      of the right cultic practices.
    attribution: The Weidner Chronicle, written c. 500 BCE
    section: prominence-during-the-early-22nd-century-bce
  - text: human face[s], dogs’ cunning, [and] monkeys' build
    attribution: A Babylonian text from the early 2nd millennium
    section: decline-from-the-late-22nd-century-bce-onwards
timeline:
  - when: c. 25th century BC – c. 25th century BCE
    what: The floruit of Lugal-Anne-Mundu of Adab, whose inscriptions — known from
      copies of c. 17th century BC — list the Guti among the nations paying
      his empire tribute.
  - when: c. 2254–2218 BCE
    what: The reign of Naram-Sin, during which the Cuthean Legend of Naram-Sin
      claims Gutium was among the lands raided by Annubanini of Lulubum.
  - when: 2200 BC
    what: Inception of the Gutian people, as Wikidata records it.
  - when: about 2147~2050 BCE
    what: The Gutians overran Akkad and, the King List says, their army subdued
      Uruk for hegemony of Sumer.
  - when: c. 2130 BCE
    what: 'Lugalanatum, prince of Umma, builds the E.GIDRU [Sceptre] Temple and
      dates the act: "At that time, Siium was king of Gutium."'
  - when: 2116 BC
    what: Dissolution of the Gutian people, as Wikidata records it.
  - when: c. 2100 BCE
    what: The Gutians briefly overrun Elam towards the close of
      Kutik-Inshushinak's reign.
  - when: c. 2050 BCE
    what: Utu-hengal of Uruk defeats the Gutian ruler Tirigan and removes the Guti
      from the country (short chronology).
  - when: c. 17th century BC
    what: The date of the texts in which the Guti first appear — purported copies
      of much older inscriptions.
  - when: 6th century BCE
    what: The probable date of composition of Ezekiel, whose Qoa some biblical
      scholars identify with the Guti.
  - when: c. 500 BCE
    what: The Weidner Chronicle is written, portraying the Gutian kings as
      uncultured and uncouth.
image-plan:
  - file: The_Gutians_capturing_a_Babylonian_city.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The Gutians take a Babylonian city while the Akkadians make a stand
      outside it — an event no Gutian source describes, drawn for an
      illustration published in 1915.
  - file: Anubanini_relief_constituents_King_Anubanini.jpg
    role: inline
    section: 25th-to-23rd-centuries-bce
    caption: 'King Anubanini of Lullubi, axe and bow in hand, trampling a foe: the
      Anubanini rock relief at Sar-I Pul, Iran, circa 2300-2000 BC.[[ ^8 ]]
      The Gutians "were close neighbours, hardly to be distinguished" from the
      Lullubi — which is as close as we get to a picture of them.[[ ^9 ]]'
  - file: Utu-Hengal_victory_stele_AO_6018_(photograph_and_transcription_of_the_obverse).jpg
    role: full
    section: decline-from-the-late-22nd-century-bce-onwards
    caption: "The stone on which the Gutians' defeat was published: Utu-hengal's
      victory stele, where he calls the people he vanquished \"the fanged
      snake of the mountain ranges\". Louvre Museum, AO 6018.[[ ^20 ]]"
  - file: Faravahar_at_Behistun.jpg
    role: drop
    section: lead
    caption: "Dropped: the file came with no caption, and nothing in the article
      says what it has to do with the Guti."
glossary:
  - term: Gutium
    gloss: The homeland of the Guti; by the mid 2nd millennium BCE the Assyrians
      and Babylonians used the name for all foreigners from northwestern
      Ancient Iran, between the Zagros Mountains and the Tigris River.
  - term: exonym
    gloss: 'A name a people is known by that is not the one it uses itself:
      "Gutians" and "Guteans" are derived exonyms for the Guti.'
  - term: text corpus
    gloss: The surviving body of writing in a language. Gutian has none apart from
      some proper names, which is why its relations to other languages cannot
      be verified.
  - term: Sumerian king list
    gloss: The list that has the Guti ruling Sumer for several generations after
      the Akkadian Empire fell, and credits Utu-hengal with defeating the
      Gutian ruler Tirigan.
  - term: year-name
    gloss: A Mesopotamian year identified by what happened in it —
      Shar-kali-sharri had a year in which "the yoke was imposed on Gutium",
      and Ur-Nammu's year 11 was the "year Gutium was destroyed".
  - term: King of the Four Quarters
    gloss: An Akkadian royal title that the Gutian king Erridupizir took for
      himself, alongside "King of Gutium", in an inscription on a statue at
      Nippur.
  - term: short chronology
    gloss: The dating scheme under which Utu-hengal removed the Guti from the
      country in c. 2050 BCE.
  - term: Weidner Chronicle
    gloss: A text written c. 500 BCE that explains Gutian rule as Marduk's doing,
      and its end as his punishment of the Gutians for taking a fish owed to
      him.
further:
  - slug: gutian-rule-in-mesopotamia
    why: The dynasty the Guti built once they had overrun the south — and the
      source of the Great Revolt against Naram-Sin, in which 360,000 Akkadian
      soldiers beat the Gutian king Gula'an at a cost of 90,000 dead.
  - slug: akkadian-empire
    why: The empire whose collapse has been linked to conflict with people from
      Gutium, though economic factors, climate change and internal strife also
      played a part.
  - slug: lagash
    why: Home of Gudea, one of the autonomous rulers who arose again in the
      city-states while the Gutians held hegemony over Sumer.
