+++
The !!Guti!! , also known by the derived [[ exonyms | exonym ]] !!Gutians!! or !!Guteans!!, were a people of the ancient Near East who both appeared and disappeared during the [[ Bronze Age ]]. Their homeland was known as !!Gutium!! ([[ Sumerian | sumerian-language ]]: 𒄖𒌅𒌝𒆠, //Gutūm^^KI^^// or 𒄖𒋾𒌝𒆠, //Gutium^^KI^^//).[[ ^1 ]][[ ^2 ]]

What brought down the [[ Akkadian Empire ]] is argued over. Conflict between people from Gutium and the empire has been linked to its collapse towards the end of the 3rd millennium BCE, although economic factors, climate change and internal strife also played a part. The Guti then overran southern [[ Mesopotamia ]] and formed the short-lived [[ Gutian dynasty of Sumer ]], overseeing a period of economic and cultural decline. The [[ Sumerian king list ]] suggests that they ruled over [[ Sumer ]] for several generations following the fall of the Akkadian Empire.[[ ^3 ]]

The name outlasted the people. By the mid 2nd millennium BCE, use of the name "Gutium", by the [[ Assyrians ]] and [[ Babylonians ]] of [[ Mesopotamia ]], was extended to include all foreigners from northwestern [[ Ancient Iran ]], between the [[ Zagros Mountains ]] and the [[ Tigris River ]]. Various tribes and places to the east and northeast, regardless of ethnicity, were often referred to as //Gutians// or //Gutium//, and the name no longer referred to one specific people or ethno-linguistic group.[[ ^4 ]] Assyrian royal annals use the term Gutians in relation to populations known to have actually been [[ Hurrians ]], [[ Medes ]] or [[ Mannaeans | mannae ]]. As late as the reign of [[ Cyrus the Great ]] of Persia, the famous general [[ Gubaru | gobryas-general ]] (Gobryas) was described as the "governor of Gutium", after which the name disappeared from history.[[ ^5 ]]

== Origin

Little is known of the origins, material culture or language of the Guti: contemporary sources provide few details, and no artifacts have been positively identified as theirs.[[ ^6 ]]

The [[ Gutian language ]] lacks a [[ text corpus ]] apart from some proper names, so its similarities to other languages are impossible to verify. What survives is a handful of royal names, and those names suggest a language not closely related to any of the languages of the region at the time — not [[ Sumerian | sumerian-language ]], [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]], [[ Hurrian | hurrian-language ]], [[ Hattic | hattic-language ]], [[ Eblaite | eblaite-language ]], [[ Amorite | amorite-language ]] or [[ Elamite | elamite-language ]]. Attempts to link the personal names of Gutian kings to [[ Indo-European languages ]] have not been accepted by most scholars, and Indo-European presence in the region is not attested until much later.[[ ^7 ]]

== History



== 25th to 23rd centuries BCE

The Guti appear in texts from c. 17th century BC, purported copies of inscriptions proposed to be of [[ Lugal-Anne-Mundu ]] ([[ fl. ]] c. 25th century BC – c. 25th century BCE) of [[ Adab | adab-city ]], which name them among the nations providing his empire tribute. The same inscriptions locate them between [[ Subartu ]] and [[ Assyria ]] in the north, and [[ Marhashi ]] and [[ Elam ]] in the south.[[ ^10 ]][[ ^11 ]] The text is fragmentary, and has been described as a "pseudoautobiographical literary composition".[[ ^12 ]]

== Prominence during the early 22nd century BCE

According to the later literary composition [[ Great Revolt against Naram-Sin | gutian-rule-in-mesopotamia#great-revolt-against-naram-sin ]], [[ Naram-Sin of Akkad ]]'s army of 360,000 soldiers defeated the Gutian king Gula'an, despite having 90,000 slain by the Gutians.[[ ^14 ]] The much later Sumerian literary composition [[ Cuthean Legend of Naram-Sin ]] claims //Gutium// among the lands raided by [[ Annubanini ]] of [[ Lulubum ]] during the reign of [[ Naram-Sin | naram-sin-of-akkad ]] (c. 2254–2218 BCE).[[ ^15 ]]

The contemporary evidence is thinner and more matter-of-fact. Year-names for [[ Shar-kali-sharri ]] of Akkad indicate that in one unknown year of his reign he captured [[ Sharlag ]] king of Gutium, while in another year "the yoke was imposed on Gutium".[[ ^16 ]] A votive macehead carries the name of the Gutian king [[ La-erabum ]] and the words "La-eraab, great King of Gutiim" (𒆷𒂍𒊏𒀊 𒁕𒈝 𒈗 𒄖𒋾𒅎//la-e-ra-ab da-num lugal gutiim//); the name is quite damaged, and was initially read "Lasiraab".[[ ^13 ]]

During the Akkadian Empire period, the Gutians slowly grew in strength and then established a capital at the Early Dynastic city of [[ Adab | adab-city ]].[[ ^17 ]] They eventually overran Akkad, and as the King List tells us, their army also subdued [[ Uruk ]] for hegemony of Sumer, in about 2147~2050 BCE. Their grip was not total: autonomous rulers soon arose again in a number of city-states, notably [[ Gudea ]] of [[ Lagash ]], and [[ Upper Mesopotamia ]] and [[ Assyria ]] appear not to have been overrun by the Gutians.

The Gutians seem also to have briefly overrun [[ Elam ]] at around the same time, towards the close of [[ Kutik-Inshushinak ]]'s reign (c. 2100 BCE).[[ ^18 ]] On a statue of the Gutian king [[ Erridupizir ]] at [[ Nippur ]], an inscription imitates his Akkadian predecessors, styling him "King of Gutium, King of the Four Quarters". An inscription dated c. 2130 BCE records the same arrangement from the other side: "[[ Lugalanatum ]], prince of [[ Umma ]] ... built the //E.GIDRU// [Sceptre] Temple at [[ Umma ]], buried his foundation deposit [and] regulated the orders. At that time, [[ Siium ]] was king of Gutium."

Writing much later, the [[ Weidner Chronicle | babylonian-chronicles ]] (written c. 500 BCE) portrays the Gutian kings as uncultured and uncouth:

"Naram-Sin destroyed the people of [[ Babylon ]], so twice [[ Marduk ]] summoned the forces of [[ Gutium ]] against him. Marduk gave his kingship to the Gutian force. The Gutians were unhappy people unaware how to revere the gods, ignorant of the right cultic practices. [[ Utu-hengal ]], the fisherman, caught a fish at the edge of the sea for an offering. That fish should not be offered to another god until it had been offered to Marduk, but the Gutians took the boiled fish from his hand before it was offered, so by his august command, Marduk removed the Gutian force from the rule of his land and gave it to Utu-hengal."

== Decline from the late 22nd century BCE onwards

The Sumerian ruler [[ Utu-hengal ]], Prince of the Sumerian city of Uruk, is similarly credited on the King List with defeating the Gutian ruler [[ Tirigan ]], and removing the Guti from the country in c. 2050 BCE ([[ short chronology | short-chronology-timeline ]]).[[ ^19 ]]

In his Victory Stele, Utu-hengal wrote about the Gutians: "Gutium, the fanged snake of the mountain ranges, a people who acted violently against the gods, people who the kingship of Sumer to the mountains took away, who Sumer with wickedness filled, who from one with a wife his wife took away from him, who from one with a child his child took away from him, who produced wickedness and violence within the country ..."[[ ^20 ]][[ ^21 ]]

Following this, [[ Ur-Nammu ]] of [[ Ur ]] ordered the destruction of Gutium, and the year 11 of king [[ Ur-Nammu ]] is remembered as the "year Gutium was destroyed".[[ ^22 ]] The destruction was not as clean as the year-name makes it sound: according to a Sumerian epic, Ur-Nammu died in battle with the Gutians, after having been abandoned by his own army. A Babylonian text from the early 2nd millennium refers to the Guti as having "human face[s], dogs’ cunning, [and] monkeys' build".[[ ^23 ]]

Some [[ biblical scholars | biblical-scholar ]] believe that the Guti may be the //Qoa//, named with the //Shoa// and //[[ Pekod | puqudu ]]// as enemies of Jerusalem in [[ Ezekiel | book-of-ezekiel ]] 23:23,[[ ^24 ]] which was probably written in the 6th century BCE.

[[ ^1 ]]: [[ "line 308" | https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=c.2.1.1&display=Crit&charenc=gcirc&lineid=c211.308#c211.308 ]]. The Sumerian King List. //ETCSL//. Oxford, UK: [[ Oriental Institute | faculty-of-asian-and-middle-eastern-studies ]]. Retrieved 19 December 2010 – via etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk.

[[ ^2 ]]: [[ "The Cursing of Agade" | http://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.2.1.5&display=Crit&charenc=gcirc&lineid=t215.p12#t215.p12 ]]. //ETCSL//. Oxford, UK: [[ Oriental Institute | faculty-of-asian-and-middle-eastern-studies ]]. Retrieved 18 December 2010 – via etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk.

[[ ^3 ]]: [[ "page 18" | http://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.2.1.1&display=Crit&charenc=gcirc&lineid=t211.p18#t211.p18 ]]. Sumerian king list. //ETCSL//. Oxford, UK: [[ Oriental Institute | faculty-of-asian-and-middle-eastern-studies ]] – via etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk.

[[ ^4 ]]: Johns, C.H.W.; [[ Parpola, Simo | simo-parpola ]] (1970). Tallqvist, Knut Leonard; Dietrich, Manfried; Bergerhof, Kurt (eds.). //Neo-Assyrian Toponyms//. Alter Orient und Altes Testament. Vol. 6. Kevelaer and Neukirchen-Vluyn: Butzon & Bercker and Neukirchener Verlag. OCLC [[ 102576 | https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/102576 ]]. Open Library OL20241301M.

[[ ^5 ]]: Oppenheim, A. Leo (2011). "VIII. Assyrian and Babylonian historical texts". In Pritchard, James B. (ed.). //The Ancient Near East: An anthology of texts and pictures//. Princeton, NJ: Princeton University Press.

[[ ^6 ]]: Bryant, Edwin; Patton, Laurie L. (2004). [[ //The Indo-Aryan Controversy// | https://books.google.com/books?id=8VnAk14pODsC&dq=The+First+Indo-Europeans+in+History+Henning+Guti&pg=PA171 ]]. Routledge. p. 171. ISBN [[ 9780700714636 | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special%3ABookSources%2F9780700714636 ]] – via Google.

[[ ^7 ]]: [[ Mallory, J.P. | j.-p.-mallory ]]; [[ Mair, V.H. | victor-h.-mair ]] (2000). [[ //The Tarim Mummies// | https://archive.org/details/tarimmummiesanci00mall/page/281 ]]. London, UK: Thames & Hudson. pp. [[ 281–282 | https://archive.org/details/tarimmummiesanci00mall/page/281 ]]. ISBN [[ 978-0-500-05101-6 | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special%3ABookSources%2F978-0-500-05101-6 ]] – via [[ Internet Archive ]].

